nchor generated from the exact Planned Single 180-Degree Turn and Reaccelerate task contract. Independent human calibration remains required."},{"variantId":"16000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001","dimension":"technicalComplexity","proposedScore":68,"anchorTier":60,"rationale":"Candidate anchor generated from the exact Planned Single 180-Degree Turn and Reaccelerate task contract. Independent human calibration remains required."},{"variantId":"16000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001","dimension":"supervisionDemand","proposedScore":74,"anchorTier":80,"rationale":"Candidate anchor generated from the exact Planned Single 180-Degree Turn and Reaccelerate task contract. Independent human calibration remains required."},{"variantId":"16000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001","dimension":"failureConsequence","proposedScore":76,"anchorTier":80,"rationale":"Candidate anchor generated from the exact Planned Single 180-Degree Turn and Reaccelerate task contract. Independent human calibration remains required."},{"variantId":"16000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001","dimension":"impact","proposedScore":72,"anchorTier":80,"rationale":"Candidate anchor generated from the exact Planned Single 180-Degree Turn and Reaccelerate task contract. Independent human calibration remains required."},{"variantId":"16000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001","dimension":"workCapacityDemand","proposedScore":48,"anchorTier":40,"rationale":"Candidate anchor generated from the exact Planned Single 180-Degree Turn and Reaccelerate task contract. Independent human calibration remains required."}],"evidence":[{"sectionKey":"identity","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8196559/","sourceTitle":"The Biomechanical Characterization of the Turning Phase during a 180-Degree Change of Direction","sourcePublisher":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":88,"claims":["The modified 505 uses a 5-meter forward sprint, one 180-degree change of direction, and a 5-meter sprint back.","This establishes the same base sequence described by the Turn / Shuttle Cut and Turn and Sprint cards; approach length and test setup do not create a second identity."]},{"sectionKey":"taxonomy","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8196559/","sourceTitle":"The Biomechanical Characterization of the Turning Phase during a 180-Degree Change of Direction","sourcePublisher":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":88,"claims":["The study separates penultimate foot contact, final turning contact, and first accelerating contact within the 180-degree turn.","Taxonomy must record planned versus reactive context, approach and exit distance, plant side, turn contact strategy, timing method, and whether the drill contains one or multiple turns."]},{"sectionKey":"anatomy","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9474351/","sourceTitle":"Biomechanical and Neuromuscular Performance Requirements of Horizontal Deceleration: A Review with Implications for Random Intermittent Multi-Directional Sports","sourcePublisher":"Sports Medicine - Open","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":90,"claims":["Horizontal deceleration is a whole-body action requiring lower-limb eccentric force, neuromuscular control, and trunk and pelvis organization.","Represent ankle plantar flexors and dorsiflexors, knee extensors and flexors, hip extensors and abductors, adductors, trunk stabilizers, and their joint actions across braking and reacceleration."]},{"sectionKey":"biomechanics","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8196559/","sourceTitle":"The Biomechanical Characterization of the Turning Phase during a 180-Degree Change of Direction","sourcePublisher":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":88,"claims":["The turning phase involves distinct braking and propulsive contributions from the penultimate, final, and first accelerating contacts.","Coach the approach, multi-step braking, plant and whole-body reorientation, then exit rather than treating the final foot as the only braking contact."]},{"sectionKey":"difficulty","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9474351/","sourceTitle":"Biomechanical and Neuromuscular Performance Requirements of Horizontal Deceleration: A Review with Implications for Random Intermittent Multi-Directional Sports","sourcePublisher":"Sports Medicine - Open","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":90,"claims":["Deceleration demand depends on momentum, approach velocity, braking time and distance, and the athlete's capacity to accept force.","Assess technical complexity and physical load directly, plus coordination, supervision, failure consequence, impact, work-capacity demand, and overall difficulty; no athlete level belongs on the exercise card."]},{"sectionKey":"load_fatigue_recovery","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9474351/","sourceTitle":"Biomechanical and Neuromuscular Performance Requirements of Horizontal Deceleration: A Review with Implications for Random Intermittent Multi-Directional Sports","sourcePublisher":"Sports Medicine - Open","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":90,"claims":["High-intensity horizontal deceleration imposes substantial mechanical and neuromuscular demand that is not captured by external load alone.","Track approach speed, high-intensity decelerations, turn side, surface, plant quality, symptoms, and overlap with sprint, jump, and COD exposure."]},{"sectionKey":"constraints","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8196559/","sourceTitle":"The Biomechanical Characterization of the Turning Phase during a 180-Degree Change of Direction","sourcePublisher":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":88,"claims":["The research protocol uses a measured course, visual target, controlled approach, and defined turn and exit.","Require a measured clear lane, high-traction surface, valid turn zone, appropriate footwear, and sufficient braking and runoff space; entry speed must match demonstrated control."]},{"sectionKey":"dosage","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8225134/","sourceTitle":"Change of Direction Speed and Technique Modification Training Improves 180-Degree Turning Performance, Kinetics, and Kinematics","sourcePublisher":"Sports","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":87,"claims":["A structured technique-modification intervention changed 180-degree turning mechanics and performance over six weeks.","The proposed low-repetition, side-balanced, full-recovery session dose is a programming inference for quality and testing; it is not a universal protocol validated by the study."]},{"sectionKey":"instructions","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8225134/","sourceTitle":"Change of Direction Speed and Technique Modification Training Improves 180-Degree Turning Performance, Kinetics, and Kinematics","sourcePublisher":"Sports","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":87,"claims":["Efficient 180-degree turning requires reducing horizontal momentum, rotating the body, planting relative to the center of mass, and producing braking and propulsive impulse before reacceleration.","Instructions must separate the last braking steps, plant, whole-body turn, and exit and must state approach speed, side, line rule, and finish."]},{"sectionKey":"safety_stop_rules","sourceUrl":"https://www.nsca.com/globalassets/about/position-statements/position_stand_youth_resistance_training---2009.pdf","sourceTitle":"Youth Resistance Training: Updated Position Statement Paper From the NSCA","sourcePublisher":"National Strength and Conditioning Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":88,"claims":["Training should be supervised, technically appropriate, progressively dosed, and performed in a safe environment.","Stop for pain, guarding, slipping, repeated overrun, uncontrolled knee or trunk motion, loss of balance, unsafe traffic, or fatigue that prevents braking before the turn."]},{"sectionKey":"programming","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8225134/","sourceTitle":"Change of Direction Speed and Technique Modification Training Improves 180-Degree Turning Performance, Kinetics, and Kinematics","sourcePublisher":"Sports","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":87,"claims":["180-degree performance reflects the interaction of velocity, technique, braking strategy, strength, force production, and neuromuscular control.","Program lower-speed mechanics before high-speed output or standardized testing and distinguish planned COD from reactive agility."]},{"sectionKey":"athlete_support","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8196559/","sourceTitle":"The Biomechanical Characterization of the Turning Phase during a 180-Degree Change of Direction","sourcePublisher":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":88,"claims":["The task has observable approach, turn, and reacceleration phases.","Athlete support should show the overhead course, assigned plant side, last braking contacts, turn target, exit direction, valid and invalid trials, and stop signal."]},{"sectionKey":"coach_support","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8225134/","sourceTitle":"Change of Direction Speed and Technique Modification Training Improves 180-Degree Turning Performance, Kinetics, and Kinematics","sourcePublisher":"Sports","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":87,"claims":["Technique modification can alter 180-degree kinetics, kinematics, and performance.","Coach support needs approach-speed progression, penultimate and final contact observation, plant-side comparison, timing boundaries, surface checks, and regression rules."]},{"sectionKey":"accessibility","sourceUrl":"https://www.nsca.com/globalassets/about/position-statements/position_stand_youth_resistance_training---2009.pdf","sourceTitle":"Youth Resistance Training: Updated Position Statement Paper From the NSCA","sourcePublisher":"National Strength and Conditioning Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":88,"claims":["Exercise selection and progression should be appropriate to demonstrated technique and physical capacity.","Scale by walking or jogging, shortening the approach, enlarging the turn zone, removing timing, rehearsing each side separately, and using visual and verbal cues."]},{"sectionKey":"alternates","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8196559/","sourceTitle":"The Biomechanical Characterization of the Turning Phase during a 180-Degree Change of Direction","sourcePublisher":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":88,"claims":["Modified and traditional 505 protocols share a single 180-degree turn but differ in approach conditions and measurement.","Treat approach distance, plant side, and timing as variants; a reactive cue, multiple turns, aerial rotation, or terminal stick changes the task enough to require separate assessment."]},{"sectionKey":"media","sourceUrl":"https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/171780?expand=PrivacyEnhancedMode&hl=en","sourceTitle":"Embed videos and playlists","sourcePublisher":"YouTube Help","sourceKind":"manufacturer_instruction","evidenceQuality":82,"claims":["YouTube supports privacy-enhanced embedding through youtube-nocookie.com.","The five direct candidates returned public oEmbed metadata during discovery. Exact protocol, plant side, full content, captions, cue and safety quality, reviewer identity, and approval remain pending."]}